.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility is the front end to the account database in the shape of
.Fx Ns 's :
one program with a command for each operation.
The same program, run as
.Xr useradd 8 ,
.Xr userdel 8 ,
.Xr usermod 8 ,
.Xr groupadd 8 ,
.Xr groupdel 8
or
.Xr groupmod 8 ,
offers the
.Nx
and
.Ox
commands instead, over the same code.
.Pp
Each command may also be written as two words:
.Cm user add ,
.Cm group show ,
and so on.
The user or group is named by the first operand or by
.Fl n ;
a name that is all digits and names no account is taken as a user or
group ID.
Options may follow the name.
.Pp
Every change is made under the database lock, an exclusive
.Xr flock 2
on
.Pa master.passwd
also taken by
.Xr vipw 8 ;
if another program holds it,
.Nm
fails at once rather than wait.
The new files are checked as
.Xr pwd_mkdb 8
checks them and installed the same way:
.Pa group ,
then
.Pa master.passwd
(mode 0600) and the public
.Pa passwd
(mode 0644).
Changes need the superuser.
.Pp
Defaults for new accounts come from
.Xr usermgmt.conf 5 .
.Ss Options for every command
.Bl -tag -width "-V etcdir"
.It Fl V Ar etcdir
Work on the database in
.Ar etcdir
instead of
.Pa /etc ,
reading
.Pa usermgmt.conf
from there too.
.It Fl C Ar config
Read defaults from
.Ar config
instead of
.Pa usermgmt.conf .
.It Fl q
Print no error messages; the exit status still reports failure.
.It Fl Y
Accepted for compatibility; there is no NIS.
.El
.Ss User commands
.Bl -tag -width "usernext"
.It Cm useradd
Add a user.
Unless
.Fl g
says otherwise, the user gets a group of its own, named like it, and
its user ID is chosen free as both a user and a group ID so the two
match.
New accounts have no usable password
.Pq Ql *
until one is given.
.It Cm usermod
Change a user.
Without a name,
.Fl u
names the user; with one, it is the new user ID.
.It Cm userdel
Remove a user, its memberships and its crontab, and its own group if no
other user has that group as its primary group.
The superuser account
.Ql root
cannot be removed.
.It Cm usershow
Print the user's
.Pa master.passwd
line.
.It Cm usernext
Print the user and group IDs the next
.Cm useradd
would choose, as
.Ar uid : Ns Ar gid .
.It Cm lock , Cm unlock
Disable or re-enable password logins by adding or removing a
.Ql *LOCKED*
prefix on the password hash.
.El
.Pp
The options for
.Cm useradd
and
.Cm usermod :
.Bl -tag -width "-G grouplist"
.It Fl b Ar base-dir
The directory new homes are made in
.Pq Cm useradd .
.It Fl c Ar comment
The comment
.Pq GECOS
field: usually the full name.
.It Fl d Ar home
The home directory.
.It Fl e Ar date
When the account expires: seconds since the Epoch, a date as
.Ar YYYY-MM-DD
or
.Ar dd-mmm-yy Ns Op Ar yy ,
or
.No + Ns Ar n Ns Op Cm hdwmy
for hours, days (the default), weeks, months or years from now.
0 means never.
.It Fl G Ar grouplist
The supplementary groups, separated by commas; the user is made a
member of exactly these.
.It Fl g Ar group
The primary group, by name or ID.
.It Fl H Ar fd
Read an already hashed password from descriptor
.Ar fd .
.It Fl h Ar fd
Read a password from descriptor
.Ar fd
(prompting with echo off if it is a terminal) and store its
SHA-512
.Xr crypt 3
hash.
.Fl h Cm -
gives no usable password.
.It Fl k Ar skel
The skeleton directory a new home is filled from.
A file named
.Pa dot.foo
is copied as
.Pa .foo .
.It Fl L Ar class
The
.Xr login.conf 5
class.
.It Fl l Ar newname
The new login name
.Pq Cm usermod ;
group memberships follow it.
.It Fl M Ar mode
The mode of a new home directory, in octal.
.It Fl m
Create the home directory from the skeleton if it does not exist.
.It Fl N
Print the new entry instead of installing it.
.It Fl o
Allow a user ID already in use.
.It Fl p Ar date
When the password must be changed, in the forms of
.Fl e .
.It Fl s Ar shell
The login shell: a path, or a name looked up in
.Pa /etc/shells
and then
.Pa /bin ,
.Pa /usr/bin
and
.Pa /usr/local/bin .
.It Fl u Ar uid
The user ID.
.It Fl w Ar method
Set the password by method:
.Cm no
(none usable),
.Cm none
(empty: no password needed) or
.Cm random
(generated and printed).
.El
.Pp
.Cm userdel Fl r
also removes the home directory and the mail spool, but only a
directory the user owns that no other account uses.
.Pp
.Cm usershow
prints every user with
.Fl a ,
the public seven-field
.Pa passwd
line with
.Fl 7 ,
and a readable summary with
.Fl P .
.Ss Group commands
.Bl -tag -width "groupnext"
.It Cm groupadd
Add a group, with the next free group ID unless
.Fl g
gives one.
.It Cm groupmod
Change a group.
Without a name,
.Fl g
names the group; with one, it is the new group ID.
.It Cm groupdel
Remove a group.
A group that is a user's primary group is not removed.
.It Cm groupshow
Print the group's line;
.Fl a
for every group,
.Fl P
for a readable summary.
.It Cm groupnext
Print the next free group ID.
.El
.Pp
The options for
.Cm groupadd
and
.Cm groupmod :
.Bl -tag -width "-M members"
.It Fl d Ar members
Remove these users from the group
.Pq Cm groupmod .
.It Fl g Ar gid
The group ID.
.It Fl l Ar newname
The new group name
.Pq Cm groupmod .
.It Fl M Ar members
The members, replacing any there are.
.It Fl m Ar members
Add these users
.Pq Cm groupmod .
.It Fl N
Print the new entry instead of installing it.
.It Fl o
Allow a group ID already in use.
.El
.Pp
Members are separated by commas and must be existing users.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /etc/usermgmt.conf -compact
.It Pa /etc/master.passwd
the account database
.It Pa /etc/passwd
its public form
.It Pa /etc/group
the groups
.It Pa /etc/usermgmt.conf
defaults for new accounts
.It Pa /etc/skel
the default skeleton
.It Pa /etc/shells
the login shells
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
The exit status is one of
.Xr sysexits 3 Ns 's :
0 on success,
.Er EX_USAGE
for a bad command line,
.Er EX_DATAERR
for a bad value, a name or ID in use, or a refused change,
.Er EX_NOUSER
for a user or group that does not exist,
.Er EX_OSFILE
for a missing or unreadable database file,
.Er EX_CANTCREAT
when a file or home directory cannot be written,
.Er EX_TEMPFAIL
when the database is locked, and
.Er EX_NOPERM
when not run by the superuser.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Add a user with a home directory and ask for a password:
.Dl # pw useradd alice -c \(dqAlice Liddell\(dq -G wheel -m -h 0
.Pp
Add a user from a script, its password on standard input:
.Dl # echo \(dq$password\(dq | pw useradd bob -m -h 0
.Pp
Add a user to a group:
.Dl # pw groupmod staff -m alice
